1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,880
Egypt. About four and a
half thousand years ago.

2
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:05,930
Fardin is standing on a
ramp of mud brick and stone.

3
00:00:06,323 --> 00:00:07,763
Above him, a block of limestone.

4
00:00:08,063 --> 00:00:09,343
Two tons. Maybe more.

5
00:00:09,955 --> 00:00:10,995
And it just started sliding.

6
00:00:12,925 --> 00:00:13,985
The workers grab the ropes.

7
00:00:14,025 --> 00:00:14,785
They dig in.

8
00:00:14,825 --> 00:00:15,585
They stop it.

9
00:00:15,825 --> 00:00:16,645
Just in time.

10
00:00:17,242 --> 00:00:19,222
Most people think slaves built this place.

11
00:00:19,402 --> 00:00:20,122
Or aliens.

12
00:00:20,871 --> 00:00:22,451
Nobody here can see him.

13
00:00:22,591 --> 00:00:24,391
Nobody would have pulled
him out of the way.

14
00:00:24,727 --> 00:00:26,587
Fardin is about to find out the truth.

15
00:00:27,411 --> 00:00:28,391
Because this...

16
00:00:28,861 --> 00:00:29,781
is what they were building.

17
00:00:30,869 --> 00:00:32,229
But let's go back to the beginning.

18
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:38,627
Giza. On the west bank of the Nile.

19
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:42,799
The pharaoh Khufu has ordered a tomb
bigger than anything the world has ever

20
00:00:42,839 --> 00:00:47,362
seen. This is the Fourth Dynasty,
around twenty five sixty BC.

21
00:00:47,782 --> 00:00:49,662
Khufu wants it finished in his lifetime.

22
00:00:50,268 --> 00:00:52,508
And everywhere Fardin looks,
people are working.

23
00:00:52,865 --> 00:00:55,545
And his watch can jump him
forward through the years.

24
00:00:55,885 --> 00:00:57,485
So Fardin can watch it rise.

25
00:00:58,407 --> 00:01:00,727
In this episode,
Fardin will see three things.

26
00:01:01,089 --> 00:01:03,209
Where these giant stones really came from.

27
00:01:03,451 --> 00:01:05,591
The surprisingly simple
trick that moved them.

28
00:01:05,877 --> 00:01:07,657
And what the workers actually ate.

29
00:01:07,877 --> 00:01:09,537
That one changes the whole story.

30
00:01:10,075 --> 00:01:11,755
Let's start where every block begins.

31
00:01:12,852 --> 00:01:15,012
Every pyramid starts with
a hole in the ground.

32
00:01:15,427 --> 00:01:19,687
The core blocks were cut right here,
from quarries on the Giza plateau itself.

33
00:01:20,072 --> 00:01:23,032
You can still see those quarries
next to the pyramids today.

34
00:01:23,373 --> 00:01:26,253
But for the outer layer,
the builders wanted something special.

35
00:01:26,859 --> 00:01:27,999
Brilliant white limestone.

36
00:01:28,419 --> 00:01:29,819
From Tura, across the river.

37
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:31,890
No iron. No steel.

38
00:01:32,308 --> 00:01:35,768
Their tools were copper chisels,
wooden mallets, and a lot of patience.

39
00:01:36,270 --> 00:01:38,430
Workers cut channels around each block.

40
00:01:38,670 --> 00:01:40,630
Then they pried it loose
with wooden levers.

41
00:01:41,107 --> 00:01:42,127
Copper is soft.

42
00:01:42,247 --> 00:01:44,527
So the chisels had to be
sharpened again and again.

43
00:01:44,856 --> 00:01:46,976
It is slow, dusty, deafening work.

44
00:01:47,096 --> 00:01:48,816
And Fardin loves every second.

45
00:01:49,688 --> 00:01:50,888
Fardin decides to help.

46
00:01:51,346 --> 00:01:52,246
He grabs a block.

47
00:01:52,949 --> 00:01:53,669
He pulls.

48
00:01:54,616 --> 00:01:55,336
Nothing.

49
00:01:56,614 --> 00:01:58,134
The workers don't even notice.

50
00:01:58,314 --> 00:02:00,414
To them, it's just another day at work.

51
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:04,430
The average block in the Great Pyramid
weighs around two and a half tons.

52
00:02:04,901 --> 00:02:08,321
And by one estimate, there are about
two point three million of them.

53
00:02:09,036 --> 00:02:11,516
So how do you move a two
ton rock across a river?

54
00:02:12,117 --> 00:02:15,217
The answer turned up in the
oldest written papyri ever found.

55
00:02:16,196 --> 00:02:17,436
The Nile was Egypt's highway.

56
00:02:17,875 --> 00:02:19,375
Every year the river flooded.

57
00:02:19,555 --> 00:02:22,075
And the builders used that
water to their advantage.

58
00:02:22,541 --> 00:02:25,301
During the flood, boats could
get much closer to Giza.

59
00:02:25,461 --> 00:02:27,081
Heavy cargo, less walking.

60
00:02:27,548 --> 00:02:29,048
Fardin tries to hitch a ride.

61
00:02:29,208 --> 00:02:30,768
He floats straight through the deck.

62
00:02:31,105 --> 00:02:35,405
Boats carried the stone across the river
and along canals, right up to Giza.

63
00:02:36,278 --> 00:02:41,298
In twenty thirteen, archaeologists made
an incredible find at Wadi al-Jarf, on the

64
00:02:41,338 --> 00:02:42,338
Red Sea coast.

65
00:02:42,865 --> 00:02:43,845
Papyrus scrolls.

66
00:02:44,125 --> 00:02:45,785
About forty five hundred years old.

67
00:02:46,273 --> 00:02:48,493
The oldest inscribed
papyri ever discovered.

68
00:02:48,891 --> 00:02:51,091
They belonged to an inspector named Merer.

69
00:02:51,331 --> 00:02:52,871
And they read like a work diary.

70
00:02:53,386 --> 00:02:55,706
They date to the final
years of Khufu's reign.

71
00:02:56,554 --> 00:03:01,254
Merer's logbook tracks his crew of about
forty men, hauling limestone from Tura to

72
00:03:01,294 --> 00:03:02,694
Giza. By boat.

73
00:03:03,098 --> 00:03:04,338
Load. Sail. Unload.

74
00:03:04,418 --> 00:03:05,578
Then back again for more.

75
00:03:05,908 --> 00:03:08,588
Thousands of tons of stone,
one boatload at a time.

76
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:09,647
Not a mystery.

77
00:03:09,707 --> 00:03:13,027
Not magic. A schedule,
a manager, and a job to do.

78
00:03:13,589 --> 00:03:16,749
But once the stone reached Giza,
the real problem began.

79
00:03:17,817 --> 00:03:18,837
Now the hard part.

80
00:03:18,997 --> 00:03:20,477
Getting the stone across the plateau.

81
00:03:20,982 --> 00:03:22,222
No wheels for this job.

82
00:03:22,302 --> 00:03:27,004
No cranes. Blocks were dragged on wooden
sledges, pulled by teams with ropes.

83
00:03:27,413 --> 00:03:29,593
Try dragging two tons across dry sand.

84
00:03:30,053 --> 00:03:31,253
The sledge digs in.

85
00:03:31,393 --> 00:03:33,553
A pile of sand builds
up in front of it.

86
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:35,690
So someone does something strange.

87
00:03:36,373 --> 00:03:37,713
He pours water on the sand.

88
00:03:38,681 --> 00:03:39,841
Fardin thinks it's a joke.

89
00:03:40,403 --> 00:03:41,123
It isn't.

90
00:03:42,136 --> 00:03:43,476
Wet sand is stiffer.

91
00:03:43,536 --> 00:03:45,716
The sledge stops plowing,
and starts to glide.

92
00:03:46,226 --> 00:03:48,926
In twenty fourteen,
physicists tested this in a lab.

93
00:03:49,442 --> 00:03:53,382
With just the right amount of water,
the pulling force dropped by about half.

94
00:03:53,745 --> 00:03:55,825
Too much water, and it gets harder again.

95
00:03:55,925 --> 00:03:57,205
It has to be just right.

96
00:03:57,537 --> 00:04:00,037
Egyptians even painted
this trick in a tomb.

97
00:04:00,297 --> 00:04:01,157
Centuries later.

98
00:04:01,991 --> 00:04:02,491
Simple. Clever.

99
00:04:02,531 --> 00:04:03,311
And it works.

100
00:04:03,821 --> 00:04:04,681
Fardin applauds.

101
00:04:04,721 --> 00:04:05,681
Nobody hears him.

102
00:04:05,927 --> 00:04:09,067
But the most surprising thing about
these workers is still coming.

103
00:04:10,005 --> 00:04:11,045
So who were these workers?

104
00:04:11,785 --> 00:04:14,305
Here is where the story you
grew up with falls apart.

105
00:04:14,698 --> 00:04:17,458
For a long time, many people
believed they were slaves.

106
00:04:17,798 --> 00:04:19,438
Hollywood loved that idea.

107
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:22,350
Then archaeologists dug
up the workers' own town.

108
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:25,060
It sits just south of the pyramids.

109
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:27,180
Today it's called Heit el-Ghurab.

110
00:04:27,454 --> 00:04:31,214
Archaeologists found bakeries,
with stacks of clay bread moulds.

111
00:04:31,352 --> 00:04:34,252
Bread and beer were the
everyday food of ancient Egypt.

112
00:04:34,653 --> 00:04:36,073
Fardin can smell the bread.

113
00:04:36,133 --> 00:04:37,173
He just can't eat it.

114
00:04:37,233 --> 00:04:38,373
Ghost life is hard.

115
00:04:39,272 --> 00:04:40,452
And meat. Lots of it.

116
00:04:40,945 --> 00:04:43,745
Researchers found huge
numbers of animal bones.

117
00:04:43,985 --> 00:04:45,285
Cattle, sheep, and goats.

118
00:04:45,739 --> 00:04:50,279
Some researchers think the animals were
raised on farms in the Nile Delta, then

119
00:04:50,319 --> 00:04:51,179
brought here.

120
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:52,770
These workers were well fed.

121
00:04:54,608 --> 00:04:55,708
Work crews had names.

122
00:04:55,828 --> 00:05:00,401
Proud ones. Inside the pyramid,
archaeologists found gang names painted on

123
00:05:00,441 --> 00:05:02,428
the stones. One of them was called

124
00:05:02,911 --> 00:05:03,951
Friends of Khufu.

125
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:07,738
Naming your crew after the king
was probably a point of pride.

126
00:05:08,816 --> 00:05:11,256
And when a worker got hurt,
someone took care of him.

127
00:05:11,501 --> 00:05:15,721
Skeletons from the workers' cemetery
show broken bones that healed properly.

128
00:05:16,061 --> 00:05:17,421
That means medical care.

129
00:05:17,810 --> 00:05:20,030
Somebody here cared
whether these people lived.

130
00:05:20,892 --> 00:05:22,352
Then came the biggest clue of all.

131
00:05:22,986 --> 00:05:26,526
The workers were buried in their own
tombs, right beside the pyramids.

132
00:05:26,666 --> 00:05:27,546
Near their king.

133
00:05:27,946 --> 00:05:31,046
Most archaeologists say that
is not how you treat slaves.

134
00:05:31,938 --> 00:05:35,758
Many were probably farmers,
working in rotating crews, paid in food.

135
00:05:36,135 --> 00:05:36,995
Thousands of them.

136
00:05:37,055 --> 00:05:39,035
Skilled, organised Egyptian workers.

137
00:05:40,149 --> 00:05:41,369
Fardin twists his watch.

138
00:05:41,569 --> 00:05:42,489
Months fly by.

139
00:05:42,569 --> 00:05:45,847
Then years. Season after
season, the pyramid rises.

140
00:05:46,632 --> 00:05:49,372
Each new layer is a little
smaller than the one below.

141
00:05:49,672 --> 00:05:51,592
The top creeps closer every year.

142
00:05:51,895 --> 00:05:54,215
But how did they get the
blocks all the way up there?

143
00:05:54,985 --> 00:05:55,745
Honest answer?

144
00:05:56,359 --> 00:05:57,659
Scholars still debate it.

145
00:05:57,831 --> 00:05:59,131
A long straight ramp?

146
00:05:59,271 --> 00:06:01,351
A ramp spiralling around the outside?

147
00:06:01,511 --> 00:06:03,971
Some even suggest a
ramp inside the pyramid.

148
00:06:04,523 --> 00:06:06,083
Every idea has problems.

149
00:06:06,343 --> 00:06:08,723
A straight ramp to the top
would have to be enormous.

150
00:06:09,639 --> 00:06:13,579
One real clue turned up in twenty
eighteen, at an ancient quarry called

151
00:06:13,619 --> 00:06:18,378
Hatnub. A steep ramp, with a staircase on
each side and rows of post holes for

152
00:06:18,418 --> 00:06:19,178
hauling ropes.

153
00:06:19,590 --> 00:06:23,170
Teams could pull blocks up a slope
much steeper than anyone expected.

154
00:06:23,594 --> 00:06:25,234
And it dates to the reign of Khufu.

155
00:06:25,958 --> 00:06:27,678
Up here, every mistake is dangerous.

156
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:28,920
A rope slips.

157
00:06:29,696 --> 00:06:30,876
The block slides back.

158
00:06:30,976 --> 00:06:31,936
The team digs in.

159
00:06:32,457 --> 00:06:33,257
They hold it.

160
00:06:33,868 --> 00:06:35,268
Fardin's heart is pounding.

161
00:06:35,448 --> 00:06:37,008
He's a ghost, and he still ducked.

162
00:06:37,318 --> 00:06:38,038
Thousands of blocks.

163
00:06:38,078 --> 00:06:39,238
Every single day.

164
00:06:39,278 --> 00:06:40,558
For about twenty years.

165
00:06:41,446 --> 00:06:43,186
At night, the crews rest by the fire.

166
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:44,510
Fardin looks up.

167
00:06:44,570 --> 00:06:46,230
The stars over Giza are blazing.

168
00:06:46,717 --> 00:06:49,377
And the finished pyramid will
not look like the one you know.

169
00:06:50,339 --> 00:06:51,159
The final step.

170
00:06:51,199 --> 00:06:51,999
The casing stones.

171
00:06:52,487 --> 00:06:55,487
Polished white Tura limestone,
fitted over every side.

172
00:06:55,727 --> 00:06:56,667
Smooth as glass.

173
00:06:57,111 --> 00:07:01,731
Each casing stone was cut at an angle,
so all four sides became perfect smooth

174
00:07:01,771 --> 00:07:02,491
slopes.

175
00:07:03,393 --> 00:07:04,673
And when the sun hit it...

176
00:07:05,303 --> 00:07:06,023
it shone.

177
00:07:06,585 --> 00:07:07,985
Like a giant mirror in the desert.

178
00:07:08,564 --> 00:07:11,124
About one hundred and
forty six metres tall.

179
00:07:11,424 --> 00:07:13,664
The tallest human-made structure on Earth.

180
00:07:14,039 --> 00:07:16,159
Its base covers more than five hectares.

181
00:07:16,479 --> 00:07:18,219
That's about seven football fields.

182
00:07:18,691 --> 00:07:21,251
And it stayed the tallest for
nearly four thousand years.

183
00:07:22,158 --> 00:07:23,718
Fardin climbs to the very top.

184
00:07:24,072 --> 00:07:26,432
Below him, the green
ribbon of the Nile valley.

185
00:07:26,632 --> 00:07:28,052
Beyond it, endless desert.

186
00:07:28,482 --> 00:07:30,982
Today, most of that white casing is gone.

187
00:07:31,322 --> 00:07:34,582
Much of it was carried away for
other buildings, centuries later.

188
00:07:34,897 --> 00:07:37,497
But for a while, this was the
brightest thing in the desert.

189
00:07:38,449 --> 00:07:39,929
And here is the strangest part.

190
00:07:40,628 --> 00:07:43,568
Cleopatra lived closer in
time to the Moon landing...

191
00:07:44,057 --> 00:07:45,337
than to the building of this pyramid.

192
00:07:47,343 --> 00:07:48,583
Then Fardin's watch glitches.

193
00:07:49,237 --> 00:07:50,377
The ground starts to shake.

194
00:07:50,955 --> 00:07:52,435
And far away, a mountain is smoking.

195
00:07:53,257 --> 00:07:55,377
Fardin has a very bad feeling about this.

196
00:07:55,833 --> 00:07:56,833
Next stop...

197
00:07:57,207 --> 00:07:58,008
Pompeii.

198
00:07:59,262 --> 00:08:01,662
Subscribe, or Fardin stays lost in time.

